[s0l0!-header][h1]Dereferencing[/h1][/s0l0!-header]

The [b]dereference [/b]operator (*) is basically an [b]alias [/b]for the variable the pointer points to.
[s0l0!-header][b]For example: [/b][/s0l0!-header][code format="cpp" ]int x = 5;
int *p = &x;

x = x + 4;
x = *p + 4;
*p = *p + 4;[/code] 
All three of the preceding statements are equivalent, and return the same result. We can access the variable by dereferencing the variable's pointer.
[note]As [b]p[/b] is pointing to the variable [b]x[/b], dereferencing the pointer ([b]*p[/b]) is representing exactly the same as the variable [b]x[/b].[/note]

